INT. SALT MINE ELEVATOR CAGE - NIGHT

A steel cage the size of a confessional hangs in a shaft, two hundred meters of dark below its floor. The walls sweat brine. A single caged bulb swings, though the cage itself is still.

CASIMIR DRAGOMIR, 40s, mining engineer, hard hat cocked over one eye, grips the control lever with a white fist. Across from him, wedged against the mesh, stands INSPECTOR LEILA FARHAT, 30s, in a borrowed orange jumpsuit two sizes too large, a laminated badge swinging at her sternum. She keeps her eyes on the ceiling.

The cage shudders. A shower of salt grit rains down on both of them.

LEILA
You said it was a routine descent.

CASIMIR
It was routine until it stopped.

LEILA
Then start it.

CASIMIR
I have pulled it eleven times.

LEILA
Pull it twelve.

He pulls. The lever clacks. Nothing.

CASIMIR
There is a stone in the guide rail, or the cable has slipped a tooth, or the dispatcher has gone to his supper. I do not know which. I know only that I am forty-six years old and the sign at the top says six persons maximum and we are two and still we are not moving.

LEILA
Do you want to hear what I wrote in my report on this shaft?

CASIMIR
No.

LEILA
That the guide rails were due for replacement in March.

CASIMIR
It is October.

LEILA
Yes.

CASIMIR
Then you are a woman who knew.

LEILA
I am a woman who filed.

A sound rolls up out of the dark below, a long metallic yawn, like a ship settling. Both of them look down through the grated floor. The bulb swings. Neither speaks until it stops.

CASIMIR
(whispering)
That is not the rail.

LEILA
Then what?

CASIMIR
Somebody is in the shaft.

A beam of white light cuts up through the grating from below. It sweeps their boots, then their faces, then settles on Casimir's chest. He raises a hand against it.

From the dark, a third person climbs into view along the guide rails, a dripping figure hand over hand: DOMINIK VESEL, 60s, a night-shift rigger in a rusted harness, a headlamp strapped to his forehead, a coil of rope slung across his shoulder. He grips the cage floor and hangs there, grinning through salt-white stubble.

DOMINIK
Forty minutes I have been under you, shouting.

CASIMIR
Dominik.

DOMINIK
You didn't hear.

CASIMIR
I did not.

DOMINIK
Of course not. You never listen when the lever is in your hand.

He hauls one leg onto the floor grate. Leila steps back to give him room, though there is none.

LEILA
Who is this?

CASIMIR
My rigger.

DOMINIK
(to Leila)
Madam, with respect, if you knew what I know about this cable you would not be standing so calmly.

LEILA
I am not calm.

DOMINIK
Then you are the first sensible one today.

He swings the rope off his shoulder and clips it to a ring in the cage wall, then to his harness, then, without asking, to Leila's belt. She looks down at the carabiner clicking shut against her hip.

LEILA
What are you doing?

DOMINIK
Insurance.

CASIMIR
Dominik. Tell us.

Dominik wipes his face. His grin goes.

DOMINIK
The cable is fraying at the third splice. Not the first, the third. It will hold a minute, maybe two. Not more.

CASIMIR
You are certain?

DOMINIK
I held it in my hand.

The cage jerks. A hard small drop, an inch, then it catches. All three go rigid.

LEILA
Move the lever.

CASIMIR
If I move it the splice may part.

LEILA
And if you don't?

CASIMIR
Then the splice may part anyway.

Dominik laughs, one short bark that echoes in the shaft. He claps Casimir on the shoulder.

DOMINIK
There. A man who understands the odds.

Casimir takes a long breath. He sets his hand on the lever, and with the other hand he reaches out and grips Leila's wrist.

CASIMIR
Hold the mesh. Both hands.

She does. He pulls the lever down. The cage lurches, climbs a meter, a second meter, screaming against the rail, then stops again so hard that her hard hat clatters off and falls away into the dark. They all listen to it fall. It does not hit anything for a very long time.

DOMINIK
(softly)
That was a good hat.

Above them, far up the shaft, a pinprick of daylight-colored lamp appears and begins to grow.
